  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWarming trend through next week, topping out near 70 F by Friday:\n\nOnce the aforementioned trough slides to our south and\neast, global ensembles depict the center of a pronounced ridge \naxis overspreading our region. This will act to warm \ntemperatures back to above normal through next week. High \ntemperatures are expected to begin in the middle to upper 50s on\nMonday, topping out around 70 F by Friday. Lows are also \nforecast to escape the twenties, starting in the 30s Tuesday, \nand increasing to the upper 40s to middle 50s by Friday night.\n\nThe NBM temperature spreads on this warming trend are very small\nand narrow, lending high confidence in the temperature forecast.\n\n\nAppreciable uncertainty remains for the next system(s):\n\nEnsembles do prog a shortwave trough to impinge on this ridge\nWednesday night and Thursday, but heights will only very\nmarginally decrease over our area, with them quickly increasing\nin the wake of the trough axis. With this initial system, models\nhave continued to trend much drier as the picture becomes a bit\nclearer in terms of surface front position. By the time\nassociated cold front with the wave reaches our area,\ndeformation and resulting frontolysis is progged to be over our\narea. This, paired with the max omega staying well north of our\nregion, should keep any appreciable rain chances quite low for\nWednesday night into Thursday (10-20% chance). Seeing how past\nsystems have trended west/southwest of our area (as we are\nseeing now), likely due in large part to drier-than-forecast air\nin our area (probably due to intense drought and very low soil\nmoisture), this seems to be the same sort of trend in this\nsystem.\n\nThe rain should come eventually, though! The first shortwave is\nonly one of several within a longwave trough pattern slowly\nshifting across the west CONUS. It's only a matter of time\nbefore the southwest flow of the longwave fully impinges on the\nOzarks, bringing decent chances of rain. That said, there is\nstill appreciable uncertainty in the exact timing as ensemble\nmember forecasts heavily diverge in the nature of shortwave\nposition, shape, orientation, etc. into next weekend. \n\nFor example, one main ensemble cluster scenario depicts the \nwave quickly moving northeast. This would bring rain chances \ncloser to Friday night and Saturday, but would bring lower rain\ntotals as the main lift with the wave stays northwest of the \narea.\n\nThe other main ensemble cluster scenario depicts a deeper\ndigging trough out west. This would slow the progression of the\nwave (pushing chances toward Saturday night and Sunday), but \nalso provide more lift for greater rain totals.\n\nWhether the rain comes as a quick frontal passage, or a series\nof waves, will depend on the exact progression and evolution of\nthe longwave pattern, which as already mentioned, is highly\nuncertain at this point in time. Trends will continue to be\nmonitored.",
